DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261107T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261206T170000
DTSTAMP:20261009T172252Z
CREATED:20261009T172252Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261009T172252Z
UID:10006959-1794045600-1796576400@historicmanassas.org
SUMMARY:Solo Art Exhibit\, "3rd Culture: Unseen Threads" by Kwaku Ofori-Yirenyki
DESCRIPTION:ARTfactory Presents “3rd Culture: Unseen Threads”\, a Solo Exhibition by Kwaku Ofori-Yirenkyi Exploring Family\, Migration\, Memory\, and Identity\n\nOn display: November 7 – December 6\, 2026\nOpening Reception: Saturday\, November 7 | 6:00–8:00 PM\nExhibition programming sponsored by Micron. \nMeet the Artist: Print\, Create & Connect: Saturday\, November 14 | 12:00-5:00 PM \nExhibit Summary\nARTfactory is proud to present “3rd Culture: Unseen Threads” a solo exhibition by artist Kwaku Ofori- Yirenkyi (ABSKU) exploring the intersections of family\, migration\, memory\, and cultural inheritance. \nThrough painting\, fabric\, photography\, relief\, pattern\, and abstraction\, Ofori-Yirenkyi traces the unseen connections between Ghanaian heritage and contemporary American cultural influences\, revealing how identity evolves across generations. \nThe faceless black figures in his compositions shift attention away from individual likeness and toward presence—gesture\, relationships\, clothing\, symbols\, and the ways people carry one another. Kente and Ankara textiles\, Adinkra symbols\, family imagery\, and Ghanaian cultural references exist alongside contemporary abstraction and Western influences. \nAcross the exhibition\, abstract fields of pattern and symbol gradually give way to more specific relationships\, places\, and family histories. This evolution reflects the artist’s understanding of 3rd Culture: traditional Ghanaian and contemporary Western cultures do not simply exist side by side. Through migration\, assimilation\, inheritance\, and lived experience\, they become interwoven into something new. \nThe unseen threads are what remain connected through that transformation—family\, ancestry\, memory\, values\, culture\, and belonging. The work asks not only what we inherit\, but what we change\, what we carry forward\, and what we ultimately make our own.\n\nAbout the Artist\nKwaku Ofori-Yirenkyi\, also known as ABSKU\, is a Ghanaian American visual artist based in Virginia whose work explores migration\, cultural memory\, identity\, and belonging through the lens of the African diaspora. Born in Ghana and raised in the United States\, he draws on Ghanaian textiles\, Adinkra symbolism\, family narratives\, and personal and collective memory. Working primarily in painting and mixed media\, ABSKU combines acrylic\, oil pastel\, fabric\, collage\, and sculptural materials to create layered works that examine how culture and identity are inherited\, carried\, and transformed across generations. \nFor additional information\, please visit the ARTfactory or contact the gallery for exhibition details. \nPress Inquiries\nJordan Exum\, Gallery Director\nARTfactory | Caton Merchant Gallery | 9419 Battle St\, Manassas\, VA 20110\n703.330.2787 | jexum@virginiaARTfactory.org | https://virginiaartfactory.org/\nGallery hours: Tuesday-Saturday\, 10 am-5 pm\, Sunday\, 12-5 pm
URL:https://historicmanassas.org/event/solo-art-exhibit-3rd-culture-unseen-threads-by-kwaku-ofori-yirenyki/
LOCATION:ARTfactory (9419 Battle St.\, Manassas\, VA 20110)\, 9419 Battle Street\, Manassas\, VA\, 20110\, United States
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://historicmanassas.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Where-Faith-Took-Root_Year-2026_KwakuOforiYirenkyi_Size-40x34-2-scaled.jpg
ORGANIZER;CN="ARTfactory":MAILTO:jexum@VirginiaARTfactory.org
END:VEVENT
